---
import { ArrowLeft } from 'lucide-react';
const { slug, back = '/learn', toolsBase = '/tools' } = Astro.props;
const loan = slug === 'loan-math';
---
<article class="page-shell section"><a href={back} class="text-link mb-8"><ArrowLeft aria-hidden="true" size={16} strokeWidth={1.5} /> All guides</a><h1 class="section-heading">{loan ? 'The math behind a monthly payment.' : 'Working backwards from a net payment.'}</h1><div class="prose-copy mt-10">{loan ? <><p>A fixed-rate loan divides its repayment into equal monthly payments. Each payment covers the interest accumulated that month, then reduces the remaining balance.</p><h2>Three inputs do most of the work</h2><p>Start with the principal P, monthly interest rate r, and number of months n. If your nominal annual rate is 12%, the monthly rate is 0.01. The fixed payment is P × r ÷ (1 − (1 + r)⁻ⁿ).</p><p>For MXN 120,000 over 12 months at 12% nominal annual interest, this gives approximately MXN 10,661.85 per month. The first month’s interest is MXN 1,200; the rest of that payment reduces the principal.</p><h2>What changes at zero interest?</h2><p>The formula has a division by zero when r is zero, so use the simple alternative: principal ÷ months. MXN 120,000 over 12 months becomes MXN 10,000 per month.</p><h2>The last cent matters</h2><p>Real payments use cents. Rounding each installment and each month’s interest can leave a small balance at the end, so the final installment is adjusted. Total interest should come from the same schedule you show or download.</p><p><a href={`${toolsBase}/auto-loan-calculator`}>Explore the auto loan calculator</a> to compare terms and inspect the schedule. The examples exclude fees, taxes, and insurance.</p></> : <><p>If a payment processor takes a percentage of your charge, adding that same percentage to the amount you want to keep will leave you short.</p><h2>The fee grows with the charge</h2><p>With a 3% fee and no fixed cost or tax, keeping 100 requires charging 100 ÷ 0.97, or approximately 103.10 after rounding up to cents. Charging 103 would leave only 99.91.</p><h2>Add fixed costs and fee tax</h2><p>Before rounding, net = gross × (1 − rate × (1 + tax)) − fixed costs × (1 + tax). Rearranging gives gross = (net + fixed costs × (1 + tax)) ÷ (1 − rate × (1 + tax)). Rates in this formula are fractions: 3% is 0.03.</p><h2>Check the rounded result</h2><p>A processor may round multiple fee components separately. The calculator uses the algebraic result as a starting point, then checks nearby whole-cent charges to find one that meets the desired net under its stated rounding assumptions.</p><p><a href={`${toolsBase}/stripe-calculator`}>Try the Stripe fee calculator</a> and switch between the amount charged and the amount you want to receive. Rates, fixed fees, and tax are all editable.</p></>}</div></article>
